{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`resolve_hierarchy_link` (`src/rebar/graph/_hierarchy.py:98`) promotes blocking links (`blocks` / `depends_on`) so both endpoints sit at a \"comparable level\", but it defines that level by ticket TYPE TIER — `_TYPE_TIER` at `src/rebar/graph/_hierarchy.py:26` maps epic to 2, story to 1, task/bug to 0. Two consequences, both reported as bugs against this story's parent:\n\nBug 1803-df54-18bb-4881: a task T and a story S that are both direct children of epic E cannot hold a direct dependency. Linking `T depends_on S` promotes T toward tier 1, `_promote_to_tier` (`src/rebar/graph/_hierarchy.py:77`) walks T's chain `[T, E]`, finds no story-tier ancestor because E is tier 2, and takes the chain-root fallback at `src/rebar/graph/_hierarchy.py:93` — returning E. The store records `E depends_on S`, an epic blocked by its own child, and the requested T-to-S edge is never written.\n\nBug jira-reb-1582: comparability should key on the parent hierarchy, not on type. Any two tickets under the same parent may hold a dependency; a cross-subtree dependency escalates to the nearest common ancestor's children.\n\nA second defect compounds this. `add_dependency` (`src/rebar/graph/_links.py:180`) rejects redundant links, but `is_redundant` is computed by the resolver at `src/rebar/graph/_hierarchy.py:167` from the ORIGINAL source and target, before promotion. `T depends_on S` is not redundant; the promoted `E depends_on S` is, and nothing re-checks it after promotion — so the guard that should have caught 1803 never sees the offending pair.\n\n## Approach\n\nReplace type-tier comparability with structural comparability in `resolve_hierarchy_link`, and re-evaluate the ancestor guard on the resolved pair in `add_dependency`.\n\nStructural rule for blocking relations: build the full ancestor chain of each endpoint, find their nearest common ancestor, and escalate each endpoint to its own ancestor that sits directly below that nearest common ancestor. Two direct children of the same parent are therefore already comparable and link unchanged. Tickets with no parent are siblings of one another, so two roots (and two tickets in disjoint trees) link unchanged. If one endpoint is an ancestor of the other, there is no valid escalation and the link is refused.\n\n`_get_ancestors` (`src/rebar/graph/_hierarchy.py:41`) is currently capped at `max_hops=2`, which truncates any chain deeper than epic to story to task and feeds the same chain-root fallback that produced 1803. Remove the cap and walk to the root, guarding the walk with a visited set so a malformed parent cycle terminates instead of looping.\n\n`_TYPE_TIER`, `_tier_of`, `_tier_of_ticket` and `_promote_to_tier` become dead once comparability is structural, and are removed. Removal is safe because all four are private to `_hierarchy.py` and referenced nowhere else: `grep -rn` for each of the four names across `src/` and `tests/`, excluding `src/rebar/graph/_hierarchy.py` itself, returns no matches. The package `__all__` (`src/rebar/graph/__init__.py:28`) re-exports only `resolve_hierarchy_link` and `compute_archive_eligible` from this module, so none of the four is part of the package surface.\n\nEscalation itself is retained, including the machine-readable redirect record composed at `src/rebar/graph/_links.py:207`. Rejected alternative: refuse cross-subtree blocking links outright so nothing is ever silently substituted. It is the more honest contract, but it breaks existing callers that rely on auto-escalation — the `link` MCP tool and the reconciler's inbound apply path, which calls `rebar.link` at `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py:502` under a fail-open `except Exception` — and jira-reb-1582 explicitly specifies escalation, not rejection.\n\nThe non-blocking early return at `src/rebar/graph/_hierarchy.py:170` and the non-graph-artifact refusal at `src/rebar/graph/_hierarchy.py:183` are unchanged: `relates_to` / `duplicates` / `supersedes` / `discovered_from` / `caused_by` still record the exact pair, and `session_log` / `code_review` still cannot be blocking endpoints.\n\n## Out of scope (deferred, not dropped)\n\nThis ticket changes what a NEW link means. It does **not** repair blocking edges already written to the store under the type-tier rule — an epic already holding `depends_on` on its own child stays on disk until repaired, because a `LINK` event is durable and nothing re-resolves it on read. That store-wide audit and one-shot repair is the sibling ticket 9ad7-d5a2-a664-4827 (`rebar link-audit`), which declares `depends_on` on this ticket because its repair re-resolves each existing edge through the resolver this ticket delivers. The two are deliberately separate changes: this one is a semantics change reviewable in isolation, that one is a store-wide data rewrite whose correctness depends on these semantics being settled first. Splitting them keeps a data migration out of the same review as the rule it migrates to.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/graph/_hierarchy.py` — rewrite `resolve_hierarchy_link`'s blocking branch to nearest-common-ancestor resolution; uncap and cycle-guard `_get_ancestors`; delete `_TYPE_TIER`, `_tier_of`, `_tier_of_ticket`, `_promote_to_tier`; widen `is_redundant` from direct parent-child to any ancestor-descendant pair.\n- `src/rebar/graph/_links.py` — re-evaluate the ancestor/redundancy rejection in `add_dependency` against `resolved_source` / `resolved_target` rather than the pre-promotion pair.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/composer.py` — two changes in `_link_dry_run`. Update the rejection wording at `src/rebar/_commands/composer.py:636`, which currently says \"redundant link (direct child)\", to name the ancestor-descendant case. Add a branch on `res.get(\"error\")` before the existing `is_redundant` branch at `src/rebar/_commands/composer.py:635`: the function currently inspects only `is_redundant` and `was_redirected`, so once the resolver returns `{\"error\": ...}` for an ancestor pair, a dry run would fall through to the `else` at `src/rebar/_commands/composer.py:643` and print \"Would create\" for a link the real invocation rejects. The non-dry-run path already handles the key at `src/rebar/graph/_links.py:177`.\n- `src/rebar/_cli/help/link.txt` — line 3 describes promotion \"to a comparable hierarchy level\"; restate it as the structural rule.\n- `docs/ticket-model.md` — the \"Hierarchy promotion (blocking links only)\" section at line 130 documents the epic/story/task tier rule; rewrite for the structural rule.\n- `docs/cli-reference.md` (line 236) and `docs/user-guide.md` (line 169) — the same promotion sentence, kept consistent.\n- `tests/scripts/graph/test_graph_hierarchy_resolve.py` — resolver cases; existing type-tier expectations are updated to the structural rule.\n- `tests/scripts/graph/test_graph_hierarchy_adddep.py` — `add_dependency` cases, including the resolved-pair rejection.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: two direct children of the same parent link `depends_on` with no promotion and `was_redirected` false.\n- Happy path: two tickets with no parent link unchanged, treated as siblings.\n- Edge (bug 1803 repro): task T and story S both direct children of epic E — `T depends_on S` records T to S exactly, `was_redirected` is false, and no epic-to-child edge is written.\n- Edge (jira-reb-1582 worked example): Epic A parents Story C and Task B; Story C parents Task D. `D depends_on B` escalates to `C depends_on B`; `B depends_on C` records directly with no escalation.\n- Edge: a chain deeper than two hops (epic to story to task to sub-task) escalates to the correct nearest-common-ancestor children rather than to the chain root, which the old `max_hops=2` cap made impossible.\n- Failure: `depends_on` between a ticket and its own ancestor is rejected with a clear error, both when passed directly and when escalation resolves onto that shape.\n- Failure: a malformed parent cycle in the ancestor walk terminates rather than hanging.\n- Regression: non-blocking relations (`relates_to`, `supersedes`, `discovered_from`) still record the exact pair with `was_redirected` false, and blocking links to a `session_log` are still refused.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `T depends_on S` where T (task) and S (story) are both direct children of epic E records exactly one edge, T to S, and no edge on E; verified by the new case in `tests/scripts/graph/test_graph_hierarchy_adddep.py`.\n- [ ] `D depends_on B` resolves to `C depends_on B` and `B depends_on C` resolves unchanged, for the Epic A / Story C / Task B / Task D fixture; verified by the new case in `tests/scripts/graph/test_graph_hierarchy_resolve.py`.\n- [ ] `resolve_hierarchy_link` returns an error for any blocking pair where one endpoint is an ancestor of the other, and `add_dependency` raises `ValueError` for it after resolution; verified by the new failure cases in both graph test files.\n- [ ] `_TYPE_TIER`, `_tier_of`, `_tier_of_ticket` and `_promote_to_tier` are absent from the tree; verified by `grep -rn -e _TYPE_TIER -e _tier_of -e _tier_of_ticket -e _promote_to_tier src/ tests/ --include='*.py'` returning no matches.\n- [ ] A four-level chain escalates to the nearest-common-ancestor children rather than the chain root; verified by the new deep-chain case in `tests/scripts/graph/test_graph_hierarchy_resolve.py`.\n- [ ] `_get_ancestors` terminates on a parent cycle instead of looping; verified by the new cycle-guard case in `tests/scripts/graph/test_graph_hierarchy_resolve.py`, which builds a fixture whose parent pointers form a loop and asserts the call returns.\n- [ ] `rebar link <child> <ancestor> depends_on --dry-run` prints a `[DRY RUN] Would reject` line naming the ancestor-descendant case and writes no event; verified by the new dry-run case in `tests/scripts/graph/test_graph_hierarchy_adddep.py`.\n- [ ] `src/rebar/_cli/help/link.txt`, `docs/ticket-model.md`, `docs/cli-reference.md` and `docs/user-guide.md` no longer describe the epic/story/task tier rule; verified by `grep -rn \"comparable hierarchy level\" src/rebar/_cli/help/link.txt docs/` returning no matches, and by reading each rewritten passage.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/scripts/graph/`, `make lint` and `make typecheck` all pass in the worktree; verified by running each.\n- [ ] `src/rebar/graph/_hierarchy.py` remains under the 800-line module cap; verified by `wc -l`."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785371892560395001,"uuid":"9e627ea0-53f8-4eed-9bc2-1b2cd947e0b9"}